<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Chapel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as White Chapel of Senusret I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White Chapel is a limestone shrine built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> for his jubilee festival, to hold the barque of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Dismantled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, its blocks were used as fill in the Third Pylon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>. Recovered and reassembled by Henri Chevrier in the open-air museum at Karnak in the 1930s, it is one of the finest examples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> relief.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-chapel.jpg" alt="White Chapel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markh at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Chapel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
